  "sections": [
    {
      "contentId": 17592186101949,
      "id": "6d4d9680-b3c5-520a-9bac-bcce98fb02b4",
      "kind": "tutorial",
      "knowledgePoint": null,
      "markdown": "We can use Cartesian coordinates to define vectors in three-dimensional space. For example, consider a point $A(2,2,3)$, and define the origin as the point $O(0,0,0)$. The position vector of $A$ is\n\n$$\n\\overrightarrow{OA} = \\mathbf{a}\n$$\n\nLet's also define three special unit vectors $\\mathbf{i}$, $\\mathbf{j}$, and $\\mathbf{k}$, where\n\n$$\n|\\,\\mathbf{i}\\,| = |\\,\\mathbf{j}\\,| = |\\,\\mathbf{k}\\,| = 1,\n$$\n\nas follows:\n\n- $\\mathbf{i}$ is a horizontal unit vector that points \"outwards\" along the positive direction of the $x$-axis,\n- $\\mathbf{j}$ is a horizontal unit vector that points to the right along the positive direction of the $y$-axis, and\n- $\\mathbf{k}$ is a vertical unit vector that points upwards along the positive direction of the $z$-axis.\n\nAll of these vectors are illustrated in the diagram shown below.\n\n![](images/ee/eee8990fb7bd078cd1788644442f90e4e37f5a232992a5069d53315d18463a9f.png)\n\nNow, we can uniquely describe our position vector using $\\mathbf{i}, \\mathbf{j}, \\mathbf{k}$ as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na &= OA^(→) = 2i + 2j + 3k\n\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "mathAcademyId": "2924",
      "stepId": "17592186101657",
      "title": "Introduction"
    },
    {
      "contentId": 17592186100678,
      "id": "002c384c-0341-5bff-b8fe-bf1525a88574",
      "kind": "example",
      "knowledgePoint": {
        "id": 17592186100860,
        "title": "Expressing a Position Vector in Component Form Given Its Graph"
      },
      "markdown": "Given the picture below, find the position vector of the point $A(1,-2,2)$ in terms of $\\mathbf{i}$, $\\mathbf{j}$, and $\\mathbf{k}$.\n\n![](images/59/59c4c67c4ae24a83375e032704b467150e6a2d2cfc1cd51e3848c993847f74ca.png)",
      "mathAcademyId": "e-4309",
      "stepId": "17592186101660",
      "title": "Expressing a Position Vector in Component Form Given Its Graph",
      "workedSolution": "Remember that in general, if a point $A$ has coordinates $(a_x,a_y,a_z)$, then its position vector is given by\n\n$$\n\\overrightarrow{OA} = \\mathbf{a} = a_x\\mathbf{i}+a_y\\mathbf{j} + a_z\\mathbf{k}\n$$\n\nHere, the point $A$ has the coordinates $(1,-2,2)$, so its position vector is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nOA^(→) &= i - 2j + 2k\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"
    },
    {
      "contentId": 17592186101950,
      "id": "ce2c9d82-6e62-54e1-8f2e-e7278751de00",
      "kind": "tutorial",
      "knowledgePoint": null,
      "markdown": "In general, the position vector of a point $A(a_x,a_y,a_z)$ is the vector\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\overrightarrow{OA} &=\\mathbf{a} \\\\\n&=\\langle a_x, a_y, a_z \\rangle \\\\\n&={\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}{a}_{x} \\\\ {a}_{y} \\\\ {a}_{z}\\end{array} \\right]} \\\\\n&=a_x\\,\\mathbf{i}+a_y\\,\\mathbf{j}+a_z\\,\\mathbf{k}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe unit vectors $\\mathbf{i}$, $\\mathbf{j}$, and $\\mathbf{k}$ are usually called the **standard basis** of the three-dimensional space.\n\nLastly, note that a vector is defined by its components uniquely. In other words, given\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{a}=\\langle a_x,a_y,a_z \\rangle\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{b}=\\langle b_x,b_y,b_z \\rangle\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{a} = \\mathbf{b}\n\t\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad\n\t\\left\\{\n\t{\\begin{array}{l}{a}_{x} = {b}_{x} \\\\ {a}_{y} = {b}_{y} \\\\ {a}_{z} = {b}_{z} .\\end{array}}\n\t\\right.\n$$",
      "mathAcademyId": "2925",
      "stepId": "17592186101659",
      "title": "Alternative Notation and the Standard Basis"
    },
    {
      "contentId": 17592186100677,
      "id": "0752b3ce-ebd3-5cb6-a601-723932d59775",
      "kind": "example",
      "knowledgePoint": {
        "id": 17592186100859,
        "title": "Converting Between Alternative Notations"
      },
      "markdown": "Write the position vector of $A(-1,-2,3)$ using the following notations.\n\n1. The angle bracket notation.\n2. The column vector notation.\n3. In terms of $\\mathbf{i}$, $\\mathbf{j}$, and $\\mathbf{k}$.",
      "mathAcademyId": "e-4308",
      "stepId": "17592186101658",
      "title": "Converting Between Alternative Notations",
      "workedSolution": "Remember that in general, if a point $A$ has coordinates $(a_x,a_y,a_z)$, then its position vector is given by\n\n$$\n\\overrightarrow{OA}=\\mathbf{a}\n\t\t=\\langle a_x, a_y, a_z \\rangle = \n\t\t{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}{a}_{x} \\\\ {a}_{y} \\\\ {a}_{z}\\end{array} \\right]} = \n\t\ta_x\\,\\mathbf{i}+a_y\\,\\mathbf{j}+a_z\\,\\mathbf{k}.\n$$\n\nHere, the point $A$ has the coordinates $(-1,-2,3)$, so its position vector is given by\n\n$$\n\\overrightarrow{OA}=\\mathbf{a}\n\t\t=\\langle -1, -2, 3 \\rangle = \n\t\t{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}- 1 \\\\ - 2 \\\\ 3\\end{array} \\right]} = \n\t\t-\\mathbf{i}-2\\mathbf{j}+3\\mathbf{k}.\n$$"
    },
    {
      "contentId": 17592186100680,
      "id": "2eb0cb53-7b5c-5bec-895d-c22d76cc1bc0",
      "kind": "example",
      "knowledgePoint": {
        "id": 17592186100861,
        "title": "Expressing a Position Vector in Component Form Given Its Endpoints"
      },
      "markdown": "Let $A(-1,4, -5)$ and $B(2,3,5)$. Find $\\overrightarrow{AB}$ in column vector notation.",
      "mathAcademyId": "e-4310",
      "stepId": "17592186101661",
      "title": "Expressing a Position Vector in Component Form Given Its Endpoints",
      "workedSolution": "We have\n\n$$\nOA^(→) = a =-i + 4j - 5k,; OB^(→) = b = 2i + 3j + 5k\n$$\n\nNow, we can express $\\overrightarrow{AB}$ as the difference of position vectors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nAB^(→) &= b - a \\\\\n&= (2i + 3j + 5k) - (-i + 4j - 5k) \\\\\n&= 3i - j + 10k \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 10\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"
    }
  ],
